WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.219
PageMotor on Hostinger with Claude Code. Hostinger has a native MCP server.

2
00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:17.851
Give it access to your account once, then tell it what you want: it provisions the VPS, sets the DNS record, and deploys PageMotor, without you leaving your editor.

3
00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:28.754
If this is your first time on a VPS, one thing trips up nearly everyone, so it is worth thirty seconds. Three things do three different jobs.

4
00:00:28.754 --> 00:00:34.037
Claude Desktop, the chat app, cannot reach your server at all on its own.

5
00:00:34.037 --> 00:00:42.721
CloudPanel is your control panel: a web page for sites, databases and SSL, point and click, not where you type commands.

6
00:00:42.721 --> 00:00:50.753
And a terminal is a plain text window connected to the server itself, which is where any command actually runs.

7
00:00:50.753 --> 00:00:57.049
Claude Code can reach the server once connected, and that is exactly what this sets up.

8
00:00:57.749 --> 00:01:06.839
Start in hPanel. Click API in the left sidebar, under Dev tools: that is a page Hostinger built specifically for AI coding tools.

9
00:01:06.839 --> 00:01:15.576
Click the Claude Code tab, then tick the tool categories you want. For provisioning a PageMotor VPS you need two at minimum:

10
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:25.229
VPS hosting, which creates and manages your servers, and Domains and DNS, which sets the A record pointing your domain at the new server.

11
00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:30.443
The others are useful but optional, and fewer tools means a leaner config.

12
00:01:31.143 --> 00:01:39.051
Then generate the token, and give it a name you will recognise. The important part: the token appears once.

13
00:01:39.051 --> 00:01:45.186
Copy it before the page refreshes, because Hostinger will not show it to you again.

14
00:01:45.186 --> 00:01:52.134
If you do miss it, delete the old one and generate another. Tokens are cheap; security is not.

15
00:01:52.834 --> 00:02:02.141
The same page shows a ready-made JSON block for the tools you picked. Replace every placeholder with the token you just copied,

16
00:02:02.141 --> 00:02:11.596
then put that mcpServers block into your Claude Code config file, at ~/.claude/config.json. Create the file if it does not exist.

17
00:02:12.296 --> 00:02:22.872
After that, you just ask. One brief covers the whole job: the plan, the CloudPanel template, the region, a hostname, and your SSH public key.

18
00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:32.773
Then, once the VPS is running, read the IP address back from the Hostinger API and create a DNS A record pointing your domain at it.

19
00:02:32.773 --> 00:02:38.023
Regions work by name or by ID, and the guide lists the confirmed ones.

20
00:02:38.723 --> 00:02:51.088
Now the trap on this page, and it is a good one. Hostinger ships a separate image called Ubuntu 24.04 with Claude Code, and it sounds like exactly what you want here. It is not.

21
00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:59.890
That image has no control panel on it, so every panel step afterwards breaks: site creation, the database and the certificate.

22
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:07.993
Take the CloudPanel template instead, and install the Claude Code CLI on the server afterwards if you want it there.

23
00:03:07.993 --> 00:03:19.869
And do not trust the ID number either, because template IDs change. Ask it to list the available templates and pick the CloudPanel one by name before it creates anything.

24
00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:31.702
For the technically curious, your plain-English brief becomes a single API call carrying a template id, a data centre id, the hostname and your public key.

25
00:03:31.702 --> 00:03:38.669
One detail worth knowing: the item id in that call is a billing price ID, not just the plan name,

26
00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:47.504
and the suffix on it picks the billing period. Monthly, annual or two-year, and the longer commitments cost less per month.

27
00:03:48.204 --> 00:03:58.570
Provisioning gets you a running server, but that is only half the job. The on-box setup comes next: the site, the database, the certificate and PageMotor itself.

28
00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:05.201
The guide has that script, and Claude Code can run it for you over the connection you have just set up.

29
00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:12.070
And the last step is the one that proves it worked. Install EP Host Check and read its report.

30
00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:21.784
It inspects the finished box and tells you what it can and cannot do, each with a plain fix, rather than you assuming the provisioning went through.

